From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 08:49:16 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] (halium) libsysutils: skip non-kernel uevent messages quietly

uevent_kernel_recv() drops any netlink message that did not come from
the kernel and returns -1 with errno EIO. Under Halium, udev on the host
multicasts its own messages on the uevent netlink socket, so every
NetlinkListener in the container (vold, netd and vendor daemons) hits
this on every device event and logs

  NetlinkListener: recvmsg failed (I/O error)

On a MediaTek phone that is a steady stream, bursts with every uevent,
and it pushes everything else out of the kernel log ring buffer.

Treat EIO as a skipped message, as init's ReadUevent already does
(0006-halium-init-ignore-non-kernel-messages-in-ReadUevent), and keep
logging every other receive error.

Change-Id: I4d3edadb97c39f6882fc570656066d7f3a8d181a
Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
---
 libsysutils/src/NetlinkListener.cpp | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/libsysutils/src/NetlinkListener.cpp b/libsysutils/src/NetlinkListener.cpp
index 3dc2e4355c..361c8787f8 100644
--- a/libsysutils/src/NetlinkListener.cpp
+++ b/libsysutils/src/NetlinkListener.cpp
@@ -57,6 +57,13 @@ bool NetlinkListener::onDataAvailable(SocketClient *cli)
     count = TEMP_FAILURE_RETRY(uevent_kernel_recv(socket,
             mBuffer, sizeof(mBuffer), require_group, &uid));
     if (count < 0) {
+        if (errno == EIO) {
+            // uevent_kernel_recv() dropped a message that did not come from
+            // the kernel. Under Halium, udev on the host multicasts its own
+            // messages on the uevent netlink socket, so this happens on every
+            // device event; skip it quietly and keep listening.
+            return true;
+        }
 #ifdef __ANDROID_RECOVERY__
         SLOGW("recvmsg failed (%s)", strerror(errno));
 #else
